      "worked": "Let $n$ be the greatest number of edges on a face. A face with $n$ edges adjoins $n$ other faces, so the polyhedron has at least $n+1$ faces.\n\nEvery face has between $3$ and $n$ edges. Group the faces according to their edge counts $3,4,\\ldots,n$. There are $n-2$ groups and at least $n+1$ faces. By the pigeonhole principle, some group contains at least two faces. These faces have the same number of edges.",
